Upgrading Dibi and Texy
***********************

.[perex]
Since version 4.1 of the Dibi database layer and version 3.0 of Texy, both libraries have renamed classes, constants and parameters, and dropped part of their API without a replacement. The package `dresscode/upgrade-deegee` holds these changes: with it, DressCode rewrites `insertId()` to `getInsertId()`, `Type::DATETIME` to `Type::DateTime`, and calls to Texy helper methods that moved to `Texy\Helpers`; for the API that is gone, it says what to do next.


Installation
============

To upgrade an application that still runs on PHP older than 8.4, install DressCode globally, because DressCode itself needs PHP 8.4 (see [Where DressCode Runs |upgrade#Where DressCode Runs]):

```shell
composer global require dresscode/dresscode dresscode/upgrade-deegee phpstan/phpstan
dresscode upgrade today
```

The second line already rewrites the code: `upgrade today` finds the data of the package on its own, needs no configuration, and leaves the style of the code as it is. [Upgrading Code |upgrade] describes the procedure and what to do with what is left. A project that already runs on PHP 8.4 to 8.6 can also add the package as a development dependency, `composer require --dev dresscode/upgrade-deegee phpstan/phpstan`, and then run `vendor/bin/dresscode upgrade today`.


What Gets Fixed
===============

Here is a repository class written for Dibi 4 and Texy 3.1, in a project on the current versions:

```php .[before]
public function insert(array $data): int
{
	$this->db->query('INSERT INTO articles', $data);
	return $this->db->insertId();  // Method `Dibi\Connection::insertId()` is replaced by `Connection::getInsertId()`.
}


public function render(string $text): string
{
	$html = $this->texy->process($text);
	return $this->texy->unProtect($html);  // Method `Texy\Texy::unProtect()` is replaced by `Texy::unprotect()`.
}


public function getColumnType(): string
{
	return Type::DATETIME;  // Constant `Dibi\Type::DATETIME` is replaced by `Type::DateTime`.
}
```

After `dresscode upgrade today`:

```php .[after]
public function insert(array $data): int
{
	$this->db->query('INSERT INTO articles', $data);
	return $this->db->getInsertId();
}


public function render(string $text): string
{
	$html = $this->texy->process($text);
	return $this->texy->unprotect($html);
}


public function getColumnType(): string
{
	return Type::DateTime;
}
```

From Dibi, DressCode fixes the constants that used to be in uppercase, in `Dibi\Type`, `Fluent`, the drivers and the static facade `dibi` (`dibi::AFFECTED_ROWS` to `Dibi\Fluent::AffectedRows`), renamed methods such as `insertId()` to `getInsertId()`, `DibiExtension22` to `DibiExtension3`, and more. From Texy: classes with the prefix `Texy` to the namespace `Texy\`, helper methods of `Texy` and `Utf` moved to `Texy\Helpers`, `escapeHtml()` to `htmlspecialchars()`, and renamed properties, constants and parameters.


What Is Left to You
===================

Where there is no replacement, DressCode reports the place and says what to do next. This covers, for example, the method `translateArgs()`, which Dibi 4.2 removed, or output modes Texy no longer has:

```
Method `Dibi\Connection::translateArgs()` is forbidden: there is no replacement; the hook is gone; the SQL is generated by `translate()`, which is final and takes the arguments as variadic parameters.
Method `Texy\Texy::setOutputMode()` is forbidden: there is no replacement; the output is always HTML5.
```

From Dibi, DressCode also reports, for example, the extension methods `extensionMethod()` and the trait `Dibi\Strict`; from Texy, the HTML4 and XHTML output modes, encodings other than UTF-8, and methods of modules that became private in version 3.2.

What a library only quietly marks as deprecated is not in the data. With the types, DressCode reports it anyway, and where the annotation names the replacement, it rewrites the code. The data does not capture changes in behavior at all.


Keeping the Old API from Coming Back
====================================

The everyday check watches for the old API too, for example in continuous integration, once the configuration adds the types and the set `deprecations` (see [Upgrading Code |upgrade#Keeping the Old API from Coming Back]). The package carries only data, no plugin, so you do not have to name it in `use`:

```neon
typeAnalysis: phpstan

use:
	- perCs           # your standard
	- deprecations
```


What the Package Covers
=======================

| library | versions |
|---|---|
| `dibi/dibi` | 4.1 to 5.1 |
| `texy/texy` | 3.0 to 3.2 |

[Upgrading Code |upgrade#One Major Version at a Time, by Hand] describes how to proceed one major version at a time.

